          title: "Why the clock runs until the ack arrives",
          text: "A client-side completion only means the HCA sent the message — not that the server's memory holds it. Stopping the clock at the last WRITE post would measure the local enqueue rate, a different quantity, and the assignment's warning exists because students overstate throughput that way. The ack is a true completion barrier: it can only arrive after the server received the done, which in-order RC delivery guarantees came after every WRITE. Cost: one control round trip (~10 µs) added to every size — negligible above 1-byte, identical in shape for all.",
